  "notes": "The ballad-and-midtempo hit factory of the 90s/2000s — 50+ No. 1 country singles, ballad-weighted at 62–125. The method that reaches the engine is a rejection of editing: \"relentless re-cutting rather than editing — 'If something doesn't quite work, we will go back and re-do it in a minute … I'll work forever on a song, until I get it right.' Model: the groove is re-tracked to get feel, not comped.\" A re-tracked groove is a played groove, so the humanize is loose-but-consistent rather than gridded, and the fill grid is long because nothing is spliced in. The melodic parameter is his casting rule, quoted: \"I've always been after the stylist, more than someone who can do vocal acrobatics — so model conversational, speech-rhythm verse melodies with narrow interval ranges rather than ornamented lines,\" which is the step-dominant interval mix, the narrow register and an octave-jump probability near the floor. Arrangement is \"verse/pre/chorus with a spoken-or-half-sung bridge and a string-augmented final chorus.\" His selection criterion is explicitly non-analytic — \"it's when I'm riding down the road in my car and it feels like it did when I was 15 years old\" — which is why no key set is authored: the entry says the tonic is set to the singer, and inventing one would be inventing. ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: high-medium). ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the humanize values, the velocity tiers, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
